---
import IconLink from '@/components/IconLink.astro'
import Link from '@/components/Link.astro'
import Math from '@/components/Math.astro'
import PageHead from '@/components/PageHead.astro'
import Subpost from '@/components/Subpost.astro'
import Layout from '@/layouts/Layout.astro'
---

<Layout class="max-w-3xl">
  <PageHead slot="head" title="Home" />
  <section class="space-y-4 rounded-lg border p-6">
    <h1 class="mb-3 text-3xl leading-none font-medium">
      Communication lower bounds for multi-party graph traversal
    </h1>
    <h2>Ciro Zhang, Darren Ho, Ivy Hawks, Ryan Batubara</h2>

    <div
      class="not-prose justify-left mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-3 sm:flex-nowrap"
    >
      <IconLink
        href="https://github.com/rybplayer/DSCCapstone"
        icon="lucide:github"
        text="Github"
      />
      <IconLink
        href="https://github.com/rybplayer/DSCCapstone/blob/9da89f9cba41413943a7dabf593077f9a22ad37e/q2_report/final_report.pdf"
        icon="lucide:file-chart-column"
        text="Report PDF"
      />
    </div>

    <p>
      This project studies communication lower and upper bounds for multi-party
      graph traversal algorithms, motivated by real systems in which no single
      agent holds the full network state.
    </p>

    <p>
      For example, consider the 2010 eruption of Eyjafjallajökull that triggered
      large-scale European airspace closures, disrupting
      <Math value="10^5" /> flights and <Math value="10^7" /> passenger journeys.
      This forced airlines to continuously re-plain feasible routes as airspace sectors
      opened and closed.
    </p>

    <p>
      We can model such situations as a communication game: the players are the
      airports, and the airspace sectors are vertices of a graph. Vertices share
      an edge if the airspaces are adjacent. Each turn, some vertices may become
      inaccessible, but only some players may know this. What is the minimum
      amount of communication necessary to make all players aware of this
      information? Is it better to use a centralized or distributed
      communication method?
    </p>

    <p>
      In this project, we use communication complexity to prove lower and upper
      bounds for such problems. These bounds can inform system, software, and
      algorithm design. For example, for some problems it may be that the
      asynmptotically optimal communication algorithm can always be implemented
      by a centralized server managing information.
    </p>

    <Link href="/report/index" class="text-foreground underline">
      View the report here.
    </Link>

    <Subpost id="index" />
    <Subpost id="index/intro" />
    <Subpost id="index/problem" />
  </section>
</Layout>
